  "problem": "Amy rolls a fair eight-sided die. The probability that she gets a number greater than or equal to $4$ or an even number is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "Let us call $G$ the event \"We get a number greater than or equal to $4$\", and $E$ the event \"We get an even number\". Then the probability of getting a number greater than or equal to $4$ or an even number is $P(G\\cup E),$ and we can apply the addition law:\n\n$$\nP(G\\cup E)=P(G)+P(E)-P(G\\cap E)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $P(G),$ $P(E),$ and $P(G\\cap E).$\n\n- There are $5$ numbers greater than or equal to $4$ among the $8$ faces of the die ($4,5,6,7,8$), so we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\n- There are $4$ even numbers among the $8$ faces of the die ($2,4,6,8$), so we have\n\n$$\nP(E)=\\frac{4}{8}.\n$$\n\n- There are $3$ numbers, $4,6$ and $8,$ that are both greater than or equal to $4$ and even. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(G\\cap E)=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G\\cup E) & =P(G)+P(E)-P(G\\cap E) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{4}{8}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
